---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

// Checks the single-tree reading of the erf, lgamma and Gamma rows (Python 3 + NumPy).
const script = `import math, numpy as np                                     # pip install numpy
def best_tree(f, a, b, depth=3, n=41):
    x = np.linspace(a, b, n)
    pool = {'1': np.ones(n), 'x': x}                         # expression -> values on the grid
    seen = {tuple(np.round(v, 9)) for v in pool.values()}
    for _ in range(depth):                                   # one more level of eml per pass
        items = list(pool.items())
        for (ea, va), (eb, vb) in ((p, q) for p in items for q in items):
            with np.errstate(all='ignore'):
                v = np.exp(va) - np.log(vb)                  # real eml: undefined where vb <= 0
            k = tuple(np.round(v, 9))
            if np.all(np.isfinite(v)) and k not in seen:
                seen.add(k)
                pool[f'eml({ea},{eb})'] = v
    target = np.array([f(t) for t in x])
    err, expr = min((float(np.max(np.abs(v - target))), e) for e, v in pool.items())
    print(f'{f.__name__} on [{a}, {b}]: {len(pool)} trees of depth <= {depth}; best max error {err:.3g}, by {expr}')
best_tree(math.erf, 0.5, 2.5)
best_tree(math.lgamma, 1.5, 4)
best_tree(math.gamma, 1.5, 4)`;
const output = `erf on [0.5, 2.5]: 988 trees of depth <= 3; best max error 0.48, by 1
lgamma on [1.5, 4]: 884 trees of depth <= 3; best max error 0.696, by eml(1,eml(eml(1,x),1))
gamma on [1.5, 4]: 884 trees of depth <= 3; best max error 2, by x`;
---

<Base
  title="The EML Complexity Census: 23 Functions Classified by Depth"
  description="A census of 23 elementary functions classified by EML depth. Each row says what its depth rests on: a Lean theorem, a construction, a short argument, or nothing yet."
  date="2026-04-19"
>
  <div class="meta">
    <span>2026-04-19</span>
    <span>observation</span>
    <span>7 min read</span>
  </div>

  <h1>The EML Complexity Census</h1>
  <p style="color: var(--muted); font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 32px;">
    23 functions classified by EML depth. Each row says what its depth rests on: a Lean theorem,
    a construction, a short argument, or nothing yet.
  </p>

  <div class="divider"></div>

  <h2>What "EML depth" means</h2>

  <p>
    The EML depth of a function f is the depth of the shallowest EML tree that computes f
    exactly. For erf, lgamma and Gamma, which have no known exact tree, this table used to record
    a depth at which an April search found a close approximation. Nothing records that search, and
    no single tree of that depth comes close (see Reproduce), so those rows are now unverified.
  </p>

  <p>
    A depth of ∞ means no finite EML tree equals the function exactly. It says nothing about
    how well finite trees can approximate it.
  </p>

  <h2>The census table</h2>

  <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-family: monospace; font-size: 0.85rem; margin: 16px 0;">
    <thead>
      <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
        <th style="text-align: left; padding: 8px 10px; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.75rem;">Function</th>
        <th style="text-align: center; padding: 8px 10px; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.75rem;">EML depth</th>
        <th style="text-align: left; padding: 8px 10px; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.75rem;">Status</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {[
        ["integers, rationals", "0", "definition", "A constant leaf. No EML node."],
        ["exp(x)", "1", "Lean-checked", "eml(x, 1) = exp(x), in superbest_positive_one_node_ops. Depth 0 is impossible: exp is neither constant nor x."],
        ["eˣ (constant e)", "1", "construction", "eml(1, 1) = e, with rational constant leaves. (MachLib allows any real constant, and there e is depth 0.)"],
        ["ln(x)", "3", "Lean-checked", "Exactly 3 on (0, ∞): log_x_depth_exact_three, and no tree of depth ≤ 2 agrees (log_x_not_depth_le_two). This row said 2 before 2026-09-12."],
        ["√x", "?", "unverified", "Said 2 before 2026-09-12. x^(1/2) = exp(ln(x)/2) passes through ln x, which needs depth 3, and no depth-2 tree is known."],
        ["xⁿ (integer n)", "?", "unverified", "Said 2 before 2026-09-12. exp(n·ln(x)) passes through ln x, which needs depth 3, and no depth-2 tree is known."],
        ["sinh(x)", "3", "unverified", "(exp(x) − exp(−x))/2. No tree is given."],
        ["cosh(x)", "3", "unverified", "(exp(x) + exp(−x))/2. No tree is given."],
        ["tanh(x)", "3", "unverified", "(e^2x − 1)/(e^2x + 1). No tree is given."],
        ["sin(x) over ℂ", "1", "construction", "Im(eml(ix, 1)) = sin(x): one node, then an imaginary part. T03."],
        ["sin(x) over ℝ", "∞", "Lean-checked", "sin_not_in_eml_any_depth_unconditional: no tree of any depth equals sin on ℝ."],
        ["cos(x) over ℝ", "∞", "Lean-checked", "cos_not_in_eml_any_depth_unconditional: no tree of any depth equals cos on ℝ."],
        ["erf(x)", "?", "unverified", "Listed at 3, as an approximation with ε < 10⁻⁶. No single EML tree of depth ≤ 3 over {1, x} comes within 0.47 of erf on [0.5, 2.5]."],
        ["lgamma(x)", "?", "unverified", "Listed at 3, as a Stirling-based approximation. The best single EML tree of depth ≤ 3 over {1, x} is off by 0.70 on [1.5, 4]."],
        ["Gamma(x)", "?", "unverified", "Listed at 3, via lgamma. The best single EML tree of depth ≤ 3 over {1, x} is off by 2 on [1.5, 4]."],
        ["finite Fourier series over ℝ", "∞", "argued", "A non-constant one is continuous and periodic, so no_tree_eq_periodic_target applies. This row said 3 before 2026-09-12."],
        ["plane wave e^(ikx) over ℂ", "1", "construction", "eml(ikx, 1) = exp(ikx). This row said 3 before 2026-09-12."],
        ["|sin(x)|", "∞", "argued", "Kinks at multiples of π. EML trees are analytic where defined (eml_tree_analytic), so none equals it across a kink."],
        ["|x|", "∞", "argued", "Not analytic at 0, so no tree defined across 0 equals it. (On (0, ∞) it is just x.)"],
        ["sawtooth wave", "∞", "argued", "Discontinuous; EML trees are continuous where defined."],
        ["Heaviside H(x)", "∞", "argued", "Discontinuous at 0."],
        ["floor ⌊x⌋", "∞", "argued", "Discontinuous at every integer."],
        ["sin(sin(x)) over ℝ", "∞", "argued", "Continuous, periodic, not constant, so no_tree_eq_periodic_target applies. This row said open before 2026-09-12; the open question below is about approximation."],
      ].map(([fn, depth, status, note], i) => (
        <tr style={`border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); ${i % 2 ? 'background: rgba(255,255,255,0.01);' : ''}`}>
          <td style="padding: 8px 10px; color: var(--text);">{fn}</td>
          <td style={`padding: 8px 10px; text-align: center; font-weight: 700; color: ${depth === '∞' ? 'var(--orange)' : depth === '?' ? '#6b7280' : depth === '0' ? '#6b7280' : depth === '1' ? 'var(--cyan)' : depth === '2' ? 'var(--violet)' : 'var(--pink)'};`}>{depth}</td>
          <td style={`padding: 8px 10px; color: ${status === 'Lean-checked' ? 'var(--green)' : status === 'construction' || status === 'definition' ? 'var(--cyan)' : status === 'argued' ? 'var(--violet)' : status === 'computational' ? 'var(--orange)' : status === 'open' ? '#f87171' : '#6b7280'}; font-size: 0.8rem;`}>{status}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--muted);">
    <em>Lean-checked</em>: the row names a Lean theorem registered with this site's claims gate, re-proved before every deploy.
    <em>Construction</em>: an explicit tree reaches that depth, and no shallower tree is possible for an elementary reason.
    <em>Definition</em>: true by the definition of depth. <em>Argued</em>: a short argument on paper, not checked in Lean.
    <em>Unverified</em>: a value recorded in April 2026 with no tree, proof or surviving search behind it. <em>Open</em>: unknown.
  </p>

  <h2>The open question: EML-4</h2>

  <p>
    Whether there are functions that can be approximated to arbitrary precision at depth 4
    but not at depth 3 is unknown. In the exact sense the table uses, depth 4 is inhabited:
    x + 1 has depth exactly 4 on (0, ∞), Lean-checked as x_plus_one_depth_exact_four.
  </p>

  <p>
    Candidates include <code>sin(sin(x))</code>, <code>exp(sin(x))</code>, and related
    compositions of EML-3 functions. Current dictionary search (depth ≤ 4) has not confirmed
    whether these require depth 4 or whether depth 3 is sufficient with the right tree structure.
    (That is about approximation. Exactly, no finite tree equals either one: both are continuous,
    periodic and not constant.)
  </p>

  <h2>Grammar comparison: G1, G2, G3</h2>

  <p>
    The EML complexity census applies to grammar G1 (EML only). Grammar G2 adds DEML
    (<code>deml(x,y) = exp(−x) − ln(y)</code>). Grammar G3 adds EXL
    (<code>exl(x,y) = exp(x) · ln(y)</code>).
  </p>

  <p>
    This post said G3 = G1 in closure at depth ≥ 3: that DEML and EXL add efficiency (fewer nodes
    for specific primitives) but not expressiveness, so the depth classification of every function is
    the same across G1, G2, and G3. The last part is false. deml(x, 1) = exp(−x) has depth 1 in G2,
    and no depth-1 EML tree equals exp(−x): each is a constant, eᶜ − ln x, eˣ − ln c or eˣ − ln x.
    Nothing on record backs the first part.
  </p>

  <h2>Reproduce</h2>

  <p>
    No public script reproduces the April census. This page pointed to
    <code>experiments/complexity_census.py</code>, which was never in this repository. The census script
    on record, <code>eml_complexity_census.py</code> in the private monogate-research repository
    (2026-04-17), measures something else: how closely a least-squares combination of all trees up to
    N nodes fits a function. The script below checks the single-tree reading of the erf, lgamma and
    Gamma rows. It builds every real EML tree of depth at most 3 over &#123;1, x&#125; that is defined on
    the whole interval, removes duplicates on a 41-point grid, and reports the best maximum error.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{script}</code></pre>

  <p>Output:</p>

  <pre><code>{output}</code></pre>

